{
  "id": "u04-l01",
  "unit": "u04-food",
  "title": "Rice & curry",
  "status": "draft",
  "tip": "There's a famous saying: **মাছে ভাতে বাঙালি** — 'fish and rice make a Bengali'. A typical meal is **ভাত** (rice) with **ডাল** (lentils), **সবজি** (vegetables) and **মাছ** (fish) or **মাংস** (meat).\n\n**ভাত** means *cooked* rice — uncooked grains are চাল (*chal*). ভাত is so central that 'Have you eaten rice?' just means 'Have you eaten?'\n\n**গরুর মাংস** (*gorur mangsho*) is literally 'cow's meat' = beef. It's widely eaten by Bangladeshi Muslims, especially at Eid, but most Hindus don't eat beef — so it's polite to check before offering it. **মুরগি** is chicken (the bird and the meat).\n\nNo verbs yet — just point and name: **এটা কী? — এটা ডাল।**",
